            body: "A decoder needs to know exactly where one character\u2019s binary code ends and the next one begins, and the standard convention (space-separated groups of 8 digits) makes this boundary explicit and unambiguous. Binary pasted without clear separation between characters, or with an inconsistent number of digits per group, removes that boundary information the decoder depends on, leading to characters being grouped incorrectly and decoded into something other than the intended original text. This is exactly why binary meant to be decoded should be generated and shared in the standard space-separated, fixed-width format, it\u2019s not a stylistic preference, it\u2019s the boundary information a decoder genuinely needs to correctly interpret the input."
