            body: "SHA-256 produces a 256-bit hash, twice the length of MD5\u2019s 128 bits, and this size difference has real, calculable security implications, not just a cosmetic difference. A brute-force search for a collision (finding two different inputs sharing a hash) becomes computationally harder in a way that scales with output size, a longer hash means a genuinely, exponentially larger space of possible hash values to search through before a collision becomes statistically likely to occur. This is a core, mathematical reason a longer hash is considered more resistant to this specific class of attack, not merely a longer number for its own sake."
